---
import FieldGuideLayout from '../../../components/FieldGuideLayout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---

<FieldGuideLayout
  title="The recipe/ folder"
  description="Three files behind cooking. A RESC v3.11 recipe database, a pointer-deduplicated cooking-narration table, and a binary item_id lookup — all wearing Konami's SZL + LZ11 outer envelope. All three formats fully reverse-engineered and round-trip-safe as of v2.4 prep."
  crumbs={[
    { label: 'Field Guide', href: `${base}/field-guide/` },
    { label: 'File Formats', href: `${base}/field-guide/file-formats/` },
    { label: 'Recipe files' },
  ]}
>
  <p>
    The cooking minigame uses its own little ecosystem of files in
    <code>data/recipe/</code>. Three files, three different inner formats —
    but all wearing the same outer wrapper. Two carry text; one is pure
    binary lookup. <strong>All three formats are now fully reverse-engineered
    and round-trip-verified</strong>: <code>recipedata.lzs</code> (step-294)
    and <code>recipemsg.lzs</code> (step-297) each shipped a dedicated writer
    during v2.4 prep, and <code>mgcinfo.lzs</code> has no text to translate.
  </p>

  <p>
    This page is the file-format spec. The actual list of dish names lives on
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/names-and-words/food-recipes/`}>the UCC food recipe slots page</a>
    — note that the UCC food slots are a <em>different</em> file
    (<code>data/ucc/names.ofs</code>), even though the player-facing
    feature overlaps.
  </p>

  <h2>The three files</h2>
  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Packed → decompressed</th><th>Inner format</th><th>Contains</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr>
        <td><code>recipe/recipedata.lzs</code></td>
        <td>1,536 → 2,272</td>
        <td>RESC v3.11, 48-byte entry stride</td>
        <td>34 records: 10 category labels, 11 recipe names, 13 "Mysterious Soup" placeholder slots</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>recipe/recipemsg.lzs</code></td>
        <td>1,576 → 3,658</td>
        <td>Custom 34-record table (stride 56) + pointer-deduplicated UTF-16-LE blob</td>
        <td>87 unique cooking-procedure narration strings, heavily reused</td>
      </tr>
      <tr>
        <td><code>recipe/mgcinfo.lzs</code></td>
        <td>272 → 520</td>
        <td>Pure binary <code>item_id</code> lookup</td>
        <td>No text</td>
      </tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2>The outer wrapper (all three files)</h2>
  <p>
    Every file in <code>recipe/</code> uses the same Konami outer envelope: a
    16-byte <code>SZL</code> header followed by an
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/lz11/`}>LZ11</a> stream.
    The header layout:
  </p>
  <pre><code>{`offset  size  meaning
------  ----  ---------------------------------------------------
0x00    4     "1.00"               version string, ASCII
0x04    12    \x00 × 12            padding (always zero)
0x10    4     file-specific tag    distinguishes which inner format follows`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    The 4-byte tag at SZL <code>+0x0C..+0x10</code> is the only part that
    varies between the three files, and it is <strong>load-bearing</strong>
    — the engine uses it to pick the right inner parser. On re-emit, the tag
    must be preserved byte-for-byte (using the default <code>b'sfo'</code>
    from <code>lzs.compress()</code> will corrupt the file):
  </p>

  <table>
    <thead><tr><th>File</th><th>Tag bytes (hex)</th><th>Reads as</th></tr></thead>
    <tbody>
      <tr><td><code>recipedata.lzs</code></td><td><code>00 73 65 72</code></td><td><code>\x00ser</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>recipemsg.lzs</code></td><td><code>73 6D 63 72</code></td><td><code>smcr</code></td></tr>
      <tr><td><code>mgcinfo.lzs</code></td><td><code>00 6E 69 62</code></td><td><code>\x00nib</code></td></tr>
    </tbody>
  </table>

  <h2><code>recipedata.lzs</code> — RESC v3.11</h2>
  <p>
    Once the SZL + LZ11 layers are stripped, this file is an
    <a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/resc/`}>RESC container</a>
    with magic <code>RESC</code> (not the more common <code>RESO</code>),
    version <code>"3.11"</code>, 48-byte entry stride. The entries hold:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>10 category labels</strong> — top-level menu categories.</li>
    <li><strong>11 recipe names</strong> — the actual named dishes (Love Cake, Mood Cookie, Surprise Pie, and 8 others, translated in step-294).</li>
    <li><strong>13 "Mysterious Soup" placeholder slots</strong> — generic fallbacks the engine fills in when the player makes an unrecognised combination. All 13 deduplicate to one shared string in the blob.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Ingredient references inside <code>recipedata.lzs</code> are stored
    as <code>item_id</code> integers, <em>not</em> as text. To know that
    "egg" is at slot N you have to cross-reference the item table; you
    cannot translate this file in isolation from items.
  </p>

  <h2><code>recipemsg.lzs</code> — pointer-deduplicated narration table</h2>
  <p>
    This file does not reuse RESC. Its own bespoke layout, fully documented
    at <code>notes/format_recipemsg_lzs.md</code>:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>34 records of 56 bytes each</strong> (1,904 bytes total).</li>
    <li>Each record carries 4 control words (16 bytes) plus <strong>10 pointer slots</strong> (40 bytes). Slots 0..1 are zero on normal records and carry raw u32 control words on the 13 spell-soup records; slots 2..9 are absolute file-offset pointers into the string blob.</li>
    <li>The blob holds <strong>87 unique cooking-procedure narration strings</strong> — the descriptive lines the engine speaks while a dish is being made ("Mix it all together", "Bake it -- done!", "Mysterious Soup is done!", etc.).</li>
    <li>The record-to-string mapping is <strong>many-to-many via pointer dedup</strong>: 34 records × 10 slots = 340 nominal references, but only 87 unique strings exist. A single fragment like <code>と</code> (offset 0x0774) is referenced by 19 different records.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    A naive "translate one fragment per slot" approach would corrupt every
    record that shares a fragment. The pointer-deduplicated writer
    (<code>src/translator/_recipemsg_lzs_writer.py</code>) builds a
    <code>{`{source_offset: en_string}`}</code> map (one entry per unique JP
    source), writes each unique EN string once into the new blob, then
    rewrites all pointer-table entries to point at the new offsets. Records
    that shared a fragment in JP keep sharing the translated fragment in EN.
  </p>
  <p>
    The narration assembles at runtime by concatenating each non-null,
    non-empty fragment in slot order, with engine-injected item names
    spliced <em>between</em> the fragments. The fragments themselves do not
    contain <code>[ITEM:N]</code> opcodes — the substitution happens in the
    engine's narration loop, which reads the recipe's ingredient list from
    <code>mgcinfo.lzs</code> and weaves them into the slot sequence.
  </p>
  <p>
    The per-fragment cap is empirically untested; the v2.4 translation
    targeted <strong>24 EN chars per fragment</strong> as a conservative
    default. JP fragments range from 1 to 14 chars; EN equivalents at the
    24-char cap leave room for engine-spliced ingredient names without
    overflowing the popup textbox.
  </p>

  <h2><code>mgcinfo.lzs</code> — binary <code>item_id</code> lookup</h2>
  <p>
    After SZL + LZ11 strip-down this file is 520 bytes of pure binary
    <code>item_id</code> data. There are no strings, no header, just
    fixed-width integer slots used by the cooking minigame to map game
    state to specific items. Nothing here needs translating.
  </p>

  <h2>Re-emit constraints</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Preserve the 4-byte SZL tag at <code>+0x10</code> per file.</li>
    <li><code>recipedata.lzs</code> entries must stay 48 bytes (RESC v3.11 stride); entry-1 bytes must pass through unchanged; the entry-0 string-blob can grow as long as the file is repadded to 0x10 alignment.</li>
    <li><code>recipemsg.lzs</code> records must stay 56 bytes; slots 0..1 on the 13 spell-soup records hold raw control words and must NOT be rewritten as pointers; pointer dedup must be preserved so shared fragments stay shared.</li>
    <li><code>mgcinfo.lzs</code> is read by offset, so its layout must not move — copy bytes verbatim.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Related</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/resc/`}>RESC containers</a> — the inner format of <code>recipedata.lzs</code>.</li>
    <li><a href={`${base}/field-guide/file-formats/lz11/`}>LZ11 compression</a> — what sits under the SZL wrapper.</li>
    <li><a href={`${base}/field-guide/names-and-words/food-recipes/`}>UCC food recipe slots</a> — different file, but the player-facing feature overlaps.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Source</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>notes/format_resc_v311.md</code> — RESC v3.11 spec (step-294).</li>
    <li><code>notes/format_recipemsg_lzs.md</code> — recipemsg layout + pointer-dedup writer notes (step-297).</li>
    <li><code>src/archive/_resc_v311_writer.py</code> — recipedata writer.</li>
    <li><code>src/translator/_recipemsg_lzs_writer.py</code> — recipemsg writer.</li>
  </ul>
</FieldGuideLayout>
